<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="long-chain fatty acid activation" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5143" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Fatty acids are often found in the cell in the activated form of an acyl-coA. Acyl-CoAs are used in the biosynthesis of many cellular products and components. In plants they are involved in the biosynthesis of membrane lipids, seed storage lipids, |FRAME: Wax-Esters wax|, |FRAME: Cutins cutins| and |FRAME: Suberins suberin|. Intracellular membranes are generally barriers for acyl-CoAs, preventing their movement within the cell. To circumvent this limitation, fatty acids are transported in their free form, and converted to acyl-CoAs while crossing the membrane by the enzymes acyl-CoA synthetases (ACS). These enzymes catalyze the esterification of fatty acids into the CoA thioesters concomitant with transport. In plant cells, ACS activity has been detected in plastids, oil bodies, peroxisomes, mitochondria, and the ER. The activated fatty-CoAs can be hydrolyzed back to free acids (see |FRAME: PWY-5148|). The Arabidopsis genome contains nine functional genes encoding long-chain acyl-CoA synthetase - |FRAME: AT2G47240|, |FRAME: AT1G49430|, |FRAME: AT1G64400|, |FRAME: AT4G23850|, |FRAME: AT4G11030|, |FRAME: AT3G05970|, |FRAME: AT5G27600|, |FRAME: AT2G04350| and |FRAME: AT1G77590| |CITS: [12177484]|. Genetic and biochemical evidence have sorted the physiological roles of some of them. The plastidial |FRAME: AT1G77590| contributes to the activation of &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; synthesized fatty acids during their exporting from the chloroplast |CITS: [12177483]|. |FRAME: AT3G05970| and |FRAME: AT5G27600| are peroxisome-localized and are involved in fatty acids &amp;beta;-oxidation (|FRAME: PWY-5136|) in the peroxisome |CITS: [12366803]|. |FRAME: AT1G49430| is required for |FRAME: PWY-321|, where it was shown to activate the cutin monomer |FRAME: 16-HYDROXYPALMITATE| |CITS: [14973169]|.</Description>
